1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,311
Computational Skepticism, Exam Prep.

2
00:00:01,361 --> 00:00:03,012
Key Terms: Delegation and Trust.

3
00:00:03,062 --> 00:00:08,456
Thirty-three terms for handing work to an
AI, and a way to read a delegation case.

4
00:00:08,506 --> 00:00:13,220
Look for the contract, the steps, the
checks and who evaluates, then name each

5
00:00:13,270 --> 00:00:13,900
part exactly.

6
00:00:15,050 --> 00:00:15,265
Hallo.

7
00:00:15,315 --> 00:00:16,856
This is Liam, in for Bear.

8
00:00:16,906 --> 00:00:20,303
Faced with a delegation case, most people
ask only who does which part.

9
00:00:20,353 --> 00:00:22,159
That names who touches what, nothing more.

10
00:00:22,209 --> 00:00:24,811
Ask instead, is there a contract, and is
it appropriate?

11
00:00:24,861 --> 00:00:25,871
Are the steps appropriate?

12
00:00:25,921 --> 00:00:27,993
Where are the checks, and who does them?

13
00:00:28,043 --> 00:00:28,523
Who evaluates?

14
00:00:28,573 --> 00:00:31,705
This film goes through thirty-three terms
for answering those questions, each one

15
00:00:31,755 --> 00:00:31,970
shown.

16
00:00:32,320 --> 00:00:35,292
Six labels sit in the corners of the
pictures that follow.

17
00:00:35,342 --> 00:00:38,589
In the book text: the course text uses and
explains the term.

18
00:00:38,639 --> 00:00:43,259
In the glossary: the term is on the course
term list, but the text does not explain

19
00:00:43,309 --> 00:00:43,533
it.

20
00:00:43,583 --> 00:00:46,555
Computed: the numbers are worked out on
screen from stated inputs.

21
00:00:46,605 --> 00:00:50,951
Simulation: a made-up run with a fixed
seed, so it comes out the same every time.

22
00:00:51,001 --> 00:00:53,698
Illustrative data: numbers chosen to show
the idea, not measured.

23
00:00:53,748 --> 00:00:56,720
Conceptual: a drawing of the idea, with no
data behind it.

24
00:00:57,070 --> 00:01:01,616
Here is a table of thirty-three terms in
four groups, all about handing work to an

25
00:01:01,666 --> 00:01:01,903
AI.

26
00:01:01,953 --> 00:01:03,627
The first group is the contract.

27
00:01:03,677 --> 00:01:06,212
It covers delegation map, handoff
condition, testable, failure routing,

28
00:01:06,262 --> 00:01:07,361
audit trail and sign-off.

29
00:01:07,411 --> 00:01:10,233
When you read a case, these answer the
first questions.

30
00:01:10,283 --> 00:01:13,680
Is there a contract, should there be one,
and is it appropriate?

31
00:01:14,030 --> 00:01:16,532
The second row is capacities as jobs.

32
00:01:16,582 --> 00:01:19,813
It covers operational form, plausibility
auditing, problem formulation, tool

33
00:01:19,863 --> 00:01:22,000
orchestration, interpretive judgment and
executive integration.

34
00:01:22,050 --> 00:01:26,375
Each supervisory capacity, each oversight
skill, must become an artifact, a document

35
00:01:26,425 --> 00:01:27,833
the pipeline actually holds.

36
00:01:27,883 --> 00:01:33,665
In a case, these tell you which steps need
a human, and whether a check really

37
00:01:33,715 --> 00:01:34,030
happened.

38
00:01:34,380 --> 00:01:36,710
The third row is before you delegate.

39
00:01:36,760 --> 00:01:40,110
It covers phase gates, delegation buckets,
verification cost, solve-verify asymmetry,

40
00:01:40,160 --> 00:01:42,830
stakes, distribution match, reversibility
and audit trail clarity.

41
00:01:42,880 --> 00:01:46,910
These help you decide which sub-tasks the
AI should take at all.

42
00:01:46,960 --> 00:01:51,670
In a case, they answer whether the steps
are appropriate, and where the checks

43
00:01:51,720 --> 00:01:52,010
belong.

44
00:01:52,360 --> 00:01:54,972
The last row is trust and the record.

45
00:01:55,022 --> 00:01:58,300
It covers calibrated trust, undertrust,
overtrust, monitoring gap, AI Use

46
00:01:58,350 --> 00:02:00,297
Disclosure, supervisory log and
hierarchical mapping.

47
00:02:00,347 --> 00:02:05,621
These are about watching how much people
rely on the AI, and writing down what was

48
00:02:05,671 --> 00:02:05,954
done.

49
00:02:06,004 --> 00:02:10,280
In a case, they answer who is evaluating,
and whether that is appropriate.

50
00:02:10,630 --> 00:02:12,357
A loan pipeline in three steps.

51
00:02:12,407 --> 00:02:15,023
The AI scores, a human verifies, a human
decides.

52
00:02:15,073 --> 00:02:17,985
That is a partition, it only names who
touches what.

53
00:02:18,035 --> 00:02:21,836
Contract, not partition means a delegation
also states a checkable handoff and what

54
00:02:21,886 --> 00:02:22,725
happens on failure.

55
00:02:22,775 --> 00:02:27,464
So the first question about any case is,
is there a contract, or only a partition?

56
00:02:27,514 --> 00:02:31,611
For example, a job seeker's plan says the
agent finds listings and she applies.

57
00:02:31,661 --> 00:02:33,092
That is only a partition.

58
00:02:33,142 --> 00:02:37,535
A contract adds what every listing handed
to her must include, and what happens when

59
00:02:37,585 --> 00:02:38,720
the agent finds none.

60
00:02:39,070 --> 00:02:41,664
Now each row gains an input and an output.

61
00:02:41,714 --> 00:02:45,778
A delegation map is the written document
that gives every pipeline step an owner,

62
00:02:45,828 --> 00:02:48,422
inputs, outputs, and a signal that it
finished correctly.

63
00:02:48,472 --> 00:02:50,185
That signal is still missing here.

64
00:02:50,235 --> 00:02:55,180
In a case, the delegation map is where you
look to say what the contract actually is.

65
00:02:55,230 --> 00:02:58,706
For example, a job-search map has a row
for tailoring the résumé.

66
00:02:58,756 --> 00:02:59,587
Owner, the AI.

67
00:02:59,637 --> 00:03:01,644
Input, the listing and the full résumé.

68
00:03:01,694 --> 00:03:02,819
Output, a tailored résumé.

69
00:03:02,869 --> 00:03:05,170
Sending the application belongs to the job
seeker.

70
00:03:05,520 --> 00:03:06,853
Red lines fill the gaps.

71
00:03:06,903 --> 00:03:11,003
A handoff condition is the signal that a
step finished correctly, so the next may

72
00:03:11,053 --> 00:03:13,216
start, such as one of four codes logged.

73
00:03:13,266 --> 00:03:14,599
That makes the work reviewable.

74
00:03:14,649 --> 00:03:16,259
It does not prove better decisions.

75
00:03:16,309 --> 00:03:20,131
In a case, the handoff conditions are how
you judge whether the contract is

76
00:03:20,181 --> 00:03:20,408
appropriate.

77
00:03:20,458 --> 00:03:21,791
Can each one be checked?

78
00:03:21,841 --> 00:03:25,941
For example, the résumé step is done when
every skill on the tailored résumé also

79
00:03:25,991 --> 00:03:27,600
appears on the job seeker's original.

80
00:03:27,650 --> 00:03:30,090
Only then may the agent start the cover
letter.

81
00:03:30,440 --> 00:03:33,099
Five officers review one case, in a
simulated run.

82
00:03:33,149 --> 00:03:35,506
Their checking differs, yet all five
logged reviews.

83
00:03:35,556 --> 00:03:38,817
Testable means a stranger can tell whether
a condition was met.

84
00:03:38,867 --> 00:03:42,428
Under the rule, all four checks ticked,
only officer three meets it.

85
00:03:42,478 --> 00:03:46,942
In a case, a handoff that only says
reviews is not testable, so the contract

86
00:03:46,992 --> 00:03:48,146
is not yet appropriate.

87
00:03:48,196 --> 00:03:51,757
For example, the rule, send listings that
fit well, is not testable.

88
00:03:51,807 --> 00:03:52,961
Readers disagree about fit.

89
00:03:53,011 --> 00:03:57,174
The rule, send listings in her city whose
title matches her target title, is

90
00:03:57,224 --> 00:03:57,475
testable.

91
00:03:57,525 --> 00:03:58,980
Anyone can check each one.

92
00:03:59,330 --> 00:04:00,816
Forty simulated cases, as dots.

93
00:04:00,866 --> 00:04:05,425
Failure routing is a declared escalation
path for when a step fails, so someone is

94
00:04:05,475 --> 00:04:05,732
accountable.

95
00:04:05,782 --> 00:04:10,648
Scores from zero point three to zero point
seven, the red band, get a second officer,

96
00:04:10,698 --> 00:04:11,570
twenty-five of forty.

97
00:04:11,620 --> 00:04:14,642
Here the check on uncertain cases is a
second human.

98
00:04:14,692 --> 00:04:18,636
In a case, ask whether the contract says
who takes over on failure.

99
00:04:18,686 --> 00:04:22,323
For example, when an applicant-tracking
system cannot parse a résumé, the contract

100
00:04:22,373 --> 00:04:26,010
sends that résumé to a named recruiter,
who reads it by hand.

101
00:04:26,360 --> 00:04:28,606
Graded testability means testability comes
in tiers.

102
00:04:28,656 --> 00:04:32,215
Top box, anyone can check a score between
zero and one.

103
00:04:32,265 --> 00:04:35,167
Middle, only a qualified reviewer can
check a citation.

104
00:04:35,217 --> 00:04:37,464
Bottom, nobody can check the word
reasonable.

105
00:04:37,514 --> 00:04:39,104
Honest documentation names the tier.

106
00:04:39,154 --> 00:04:44,353
In a case, ask who is qualified to do each
check, anyone, an expert, or nobody.

107
00:04:44,403 --> 00:04:48,289
For example, the cover letter names the
right company, anyone can check.

108
00:04:48,339 --> 00:04:52,226
The résumé describes lab methods
accurately, only someone in that field can

109
00:04:52,276 --> 00:04:52,554
check.

110
00:04:52,604 --> 00:04:54,850
The letter sounds enthusiastic, nobody can
check.

111
00:04:55,200 --> 00:04:57,508
These are the eight items of a delegation
map.

112
00:04:57,558 --> 00:05:01,177
A broken pipeline lacks one of items one
to four, so it cannot run.

113
00:05:01,227 --> 00:05:05,107
In this made-up example, item three is
crossed out and zero of one hundred twenty

114
00:05:05,157 --> 00:05:05,631
cases run.

115
00:05:05,681 --> 00:05:09,299
In a case, a contract missing one of these
basics shows up at once.

116
00:05:09,349 --> 00:05:12,444
For example, nothing says what file the
agent's tailored résumé must be.

117
00:05:12,494 --> 00:05:16,636
The upload step rejects it, no application
goes out, and the job seeker sees the gap

118
00:05:16,686 --> 00:05:17,160
that day.

119
00:05:17,510 --> 00:05:19,002
Now item six is missing.

120
00:05:19,052 --> 00:05:22,702
An unsupervised pipeline lacks one of
items five to eight, the supervisory

121
00:05:22,752 --> 00:05:23,935
additions, yet it runs.

122
00:05:23,985 --> 00:05:27,018
All one hundred twenty cases go through,
no error raised.

123
00:05:27,068 --> 00:05:29,793
Seven red squares are wrong answers, found
later downstream.

124
00:05:29,843 --> 00:05:31,952
This is the case to watch for.

125
00:05:32,002 --> 00:05:35,652
The steps run, but a supervisory addition
is missing, and nobody notices.

126
00:05:35,702 --> 00:05:39,352
For example, an agent sends applications
all week with no failure routing.

127
00:05:39,402 --> 00:05:42,435
When it answers a screening question
wrongly, nobody is told.

128
00:05:42,485 --> 00:05:45,210
The job seeker finds out when the
rejections arrive.

129
00:05:45,560 --> 00:05:46,584
Item seven is highlighted.

130
00:05:46,634 --> 00:05:50,614
The audit trail is the stored history of
inputs, decisions and outputs that lets a

131
00:05:50,664 --> 00:05:51,688
case be rebuilt afterwards.

132
00:05:51,738 --> 00:05:54,912
The outlined square traces from input, to
score, to officer, to code.

133
00:05:54,962 --> 00:05:56,255
Without it, no way back.

134
00:05:56,305 --> 00:06:00,284
In a case, the audit trail answers whether
anyone can go back and check what

135
00:06:00,334 --> 00:06:00,553
happened.

136
00:06:00,603 --> 00:06:04,582
For example, a job seeker gets a rejection
and wants to know what was sent.

137
00:06:04,632 --> 00:06:08,611
The trail shows the listing, the résumé
version, the cover letter and when it went

138
00:06:08,661 --> 00:06:08,880
out.

139
00:06:09,230 --> 00:06:10,426
Item eight is highlighted.

140
00:06:10,476 --> 00:06:14,785
Sign-off authority is a named human who is
accountable for what the pipeline puts

141
00:06:14,835 --> 00:06:15,096
out.

142
00:06:15,146 --> 00:06:16,964
The audit trail says what happened.

143
00:06:17,014 --> 00:06:20,701
Sign-off authority says who answers for
it, including those seven wrong cases.

144
00:06:20,751 --> 00:06:25,372
In a case, it tells you who evaluates the
final output, and whether anyone is

145
00:06:25,422 --> 00:06:26,306
accountable at all.

146
00:06:26,356 --> 00:06:29,419
For example, a company's résumé screener
produces a weekly shortlist.

147
00:06:29,469 --> 00:06:33,779
A named hiring manager signs it, and
answers for any strong applicant it left

148
00:06:33,829 --> 00:06:34,090
out.

149
00:06:34,440 --> 00:06:36,887
This table compares the paperwork of two
teams.

150
00:06:36,937 --> 00:06:40,009
Operational form is the concrete,
documented version of a supervisory

151
00:06:40,059 --> 00:06:40,321
capacity.

152
00:06:40,371 --> 00:06:41,569
No artifact, no capacity.

153
00:06:41,619 --> 00:06:45,627
Plausibility auditing asks whether an
output could be true of the world it

154
00:06:45,677 --> 00:06:47,188
describes, with a written checklist.

155
00:06:47,238 --> 00:06:49,061
Only team two has that log.

156
00:06:49,111 --> 00:06:52,807
In a case, a check counts only if it
leaves an artifact.

157
00:06:52,857 --> 00:06:57,489
For example, a job seeker who says she
glances at each tailored résumé has no

158
00:06:57,539 --> 00:06:58,113
operational form.

159
00:06:58,163 --> 00:07:02,796
A checklist she fills in, does every title
and date match her real history, is

160
00:07:02,846 --> 00:07:03,420
plausibility auditing.

161
00:07:03,770 --> 00:07:04,845
Next row, problem formulation.

162
00:07:04,895 --> 00:07:09,066
It means writing down the question, the
criteria, and when AI is the right tool,

163
00:07:09,116 --> 00:07:10,472
before the AI is called.

164
00:07:10,522 --> 00:07:12,160
Its artifact is a scoping document.

165
00:07:12,210 --> 00:07:14,693
Without one, the pipeline solves the wrong
problem, fluently.

166
00:07:14,743 --> 00:07:19,757
In a case, it is the human step that asks
whether AI should be in the pipeline at

167
00:07:19,807 --> 00:07:20,038
all.

168
00:07:20,088 --> 00:07:24,258
For example, before turning the agent on,
a job seeker writes which roles she wants,

169
00:07:24,308 --> 00:07:28,760
what counts as a fit, and that notes to
former colleagues stay hers, not the AI's.

170
00:07:29,110 --> 00:07:33,197
The last row is tool orchestration,
deciding which tool owns each step and how

171
00:07:33,247 --> 00:07:34,378
work passes between tools.

172
00:07:34,428 --> 00:07:36,447
Made concrete, it is the delegation map.

173
00:07:36,497 --> 00:07:40,879
In the tally, team one has zero of three,
team two has three of three.

174
00:07:40,929 --> 00:07:45,015
In a case, tool orchestration is the
written answer to who does each step.

175
00:07:45,065 --> 00:07:49,152
For example, a search tool pulls listings,
a language model tailors the résumé, and

176
00:07:49,202 --> 00:07:51,220
only the job seeker's own account submits.

177
00:07:51,270 --> 00:07:54,470
Writing that down, with what passes
between them, is tool orchestration.

178
00:07:54,820 --> 00:07:56,754
A research paper summarizer in six steps.

179
00:07:56,804 --> 00:08:00,154
The dark rows, steps one, four and six,
belong to a human.

180
00:08:00,204 --> 00:08:03,838
Irreducibly human means a step the AI
cannot take, because it needs domain

181
00:08:03,888 --> 00:08:05,255
knowledge, domain judgment or
responsibility.

182
00:08:05,305 --> 00:08:10,072
In a case, ask whether each step like this
stays with a human, or has quietly passed

183
00:08:10,122 --> 00:08:10,922
to the AI.

184
00:08:10,972 --> 00:08:15,173
For example, an agent can fill in an
application, but only the job seeker can

185
00:08:15,223 --> 00:08:17,723
confirm that a skill it lists is really
hers.

186
00:08:17,773 --> 00:08:20,840
That takes knowing her own work, and
answering for the claim.

187
00:08:21,190 --> 00:08:22,263
Step six is highlighted.

188
00:08:22,313 --> 00:08:25,913
Interpretive judgment means taking the
output in the setting where it will be

189
00:08:25,963 --> 00:08:28,720
used, and writing down what it means for
the decision.

190
00:08:28,770 --> 00:08:32,089
The human goes through the summary as its
user would, then signs.

191
00:08:32,139 --> 00:08:36,300
Here the final evaluator is a human, which
fits, because the meaning depends on the

192
00:08:36,350 --> 00:08:36,581
setting.

193
00:08:36,631 --> 00:08:39,950
For example, a salary estimate says an
offer is below the market.

194
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:44,442
The job seeker weighs that against her own
situation, such as a smaller city and a

195
00:08:44,492 --> 00:08:47,530
remote option, and writes down whether it
means negotiate or accept.

196
00:08:47,880 --> 00:08:50,227
A dashed seventh row appears, with no
step.

197
00:08:50,277 --> 00:08:53,522
Executive integration means combining
outputs from several tools and people into

198
00:08:53,572 --> 00:08:55,020
one decision, with named authority.

199
00:08:55,070 --> 00:08:59,214
Here, one researcher runs the summarizer,
another relies on it, and that handoff is

200
00:08:59,264 --> 00:08:59,513
undocumented.

201
00:08:59,563 --> 00:09:02,210
In a case, this is the gap to spot.

202
00:09:02,260 --> 00:09:05,205
When work crosses people, who decides, and
on whose authority?

203
00:09:05,255 --> 00:09:10,298
For example, the fit score says apply, a
mentor says the role is a step back, and

204
00:09:10,348 --> 00:09:11,796
the salary estimate is low.

205
00:09:11,846 --> 00:09:15,990
The job seeker is named as the one who
weighs them together and decides.

206
00:09:16,340 --> 00:09:18,076
Red flags appear on AI steps.

207
00:09:18,126 --> 00:09:21,945
Failure visibility means saying so with a
flag, like no citation, when something

208
00:09:21,995 --> 00:09:24,623
cannot be done, instead of inventing a
plausible answer.

209
00:09:24,673 --> 00:09:27,897
Below, four of twenty entries get spot-
checked, one in five.

210
00:09:27,947 --> 00:09:32,064
So the AI flags its own gaps, and a human
spot-check backs it up.

211
00:09:32,114 --> 00:09:34,742
In a case, ask whether either check is
missing.

212
00:09:34,792 --> 00:09:38,909
For example, an agent that cannot find the
hiring manager's name flags the cover

213
00:09:38,959 --> 00:09:42,480
letter, name not found, instead of
addressing it to an invented person.

214
00:09:42,830 --> 00:09:45,465
A grid of sub-tasks against five
questions, with made-up marks.

215
00:09:45,515 --> 00:09:46,539
Red means high risk.

216
00:09:46,589 --> 00:09:49,761
Phase gates say who does what, task by
task, AI or human.

217
00:09:49,811 --> 00:09:52,178
Five questions, the course's own
heuristic, set each gate.

218
00:09:52,228 --> 00:09:54,594
When you read a case, look for the gates.

219
00:09:54,644 --> 00:09:56,743
Where are the checks, and who does them?

220
00:09:56,793 --> 00:10:00,233
Is it appropriate that there is no human
check, or no AI check?

221
00:10:00,283 --> 00:10:02,113
What exactly does each check look at?

222
00:10:02,163 --> 00:10:05,872
For example, in ranking twenty of two
hundred CVs, the AI ranks, then a

223
00:10:05,922 --> 00:10:08,020
recruiter checks the shortlist before
anyone is called.

224
00:10:08,370 --> 00:10:10,159
Each sub-task now gets a label.

225
00:10:10,209 --> 00:10:14,451
Delegation buckets are the three outcomes
of the sort, AI executes, human only, or

226
00:10:14,501 --> 00:10:15,677
hybrid with human checks.

227
00:10:15,727 --> 00:10:17,823
Wiring a payment lands in human only.

228
00:10:17,873 --> 00:10:20,275
The counting rule is invented for this
picture.

229
00:10:20,325 --> 00:10:24,873
In a case, ask which bucket each step sits
in, and whether that is appropriate.

230
00:10:24,923 --> 00:10:27,326
For example, finding listings could be AI
executes.

231
00:10:27,376 --> 00:10:31,311
Tailoring a résumé could be hybrid, with
the job seeker checking each one.

232
00:10:31,361 --> 00:10:33,150
Accepting an offer is human only.

233
00:10:33,500 --> 00:10:36,936
Bars compare minutes to make with minutes
to check, made-up numbers.

234
00:10:36,986 --> 00:10:39,788
Verification cost is how costly that human
check is.

235
00:10:39,838 --> 00:10:41,690
Four minutes against forty is cheap.

236
00:10:41,740 --> 00:10:45,809
Thirty against twenty-five is nearly a
redo, and that quietly pushes people to

237
00:10:45,859 --> 00:10:46,126
accept.

238
00:10:46,176 --> 00:10:49,295
In a case, ask what exactly the human
check costs.

239
00:10:49,345 --> 00:10:53,098
For example, checking that a tailored
résumé still names the right employers

240
00:10:53,148 --> 00:10:54,049
takes a glance.

241
00:10:54,099 --> 00:10:57,852
Checking that a cover letter describes a
company's products correctly can mean

242
00:10:57,902 --> 00:11:00,070
researching the company again, nearly a
redo.

243
00:11:00,420 --> 00:11:01,659
A number to factor.

244
00:11:01,709 --> 00:11:05,525
Finding a factor by trial division takes
three thousand nine hundred fifty-three

245
00:11:05,575 --> 00:11:06,492
divisions, counted exactly.

246
00:11:06,542 --> 00:11:08,747
Checking a claimed answer takes one
multiplication.

247
00:11:08,797 --> 00:11:12,936
Solve-verify asymmetry means checking is
easier than finding, which is why AI help

248
00:11:12,986 --> 00:11:13,902
is worth having.

249
00:11:13,952 --> 00:11:19,057
In a case, a cheap check on an AI step
counts in favor of delegating it.

250
00:11:19,107 --> 00:11:23,246
For example, finding listings that fit can
take hours across many job boards.

251
00:11:23,296 --> 00:11:26,790
Checking a listing the agent found takes
reading just that listing.

252
00:11:27,140 --> 00:11:29,956
Bars for two made-up tasks, both ninety-
eight percent accurate.

253
00:11:30,006 --> 00:11:31,961
Stakes is the cost of being wrong.

254
00:11:32,011 --> 00:11:35,687
A wrong answer costs one on task A, five
hundred on task B.

255
00:11:35,737 --> 00:11:38,839
When one error is catastrophic, the
average is the wrong statistic.

256
00:11:38,889 --> 00:11:43,424
In a case, high stakes argue for a human
check, even when the accuracy looks the

257
00:11:43,474 --> 00:11:43,710
same.

258
00:11:43,760 --> 00:11:48,009
For example, an agent that files a listing
in the wrong folder costs almost nothing.

259
00:11:48,059 --> 00:11:52,593
An agent that tells an employer the wrong
start date can cost the job seeker the

260
00:11:52,643 --> 00:11:52,880
offer.

261
00:11:53,230 --> 00:11:54,342
Grey curve, the truth.

262
00:11:54,392 --> 00:11:57,248
Black line, a model fitted only inside the
pink band.

263
00:11:57,298 --> 00:12:01,026
Distribution match asks whether an input
resembles what the AI was trained on.

264
00:12:01,076 --> 00:12:03,641
Inside the band the error is zero point
one.

265
00:12:03,691 --> 00:12:04,513
Far outside, six.

266
00:12:04,563 --> 00:12:08,872
In a case, inputs unlike the training data
are where a check is needed most.

267
00:12:08,922 --> 00:12:12,649
For example, a fit-scoring model trained
on software job listings is handed a

268
00:12:12,699 --> 00:12:14,102
listing for a museum curator.

269
00:12:14,152 --> 00:12:17,880
That input falls outside what it learned,
so its score needs a check.

270
00:12:18,230 --> 00:12:20,422
Reversibility asks whether an action can
be undone.

271
00:12:20,472 --> 00:12:23,785
In the table, a draft or an edit can, so
no gate.

272
00:12:23,835 --> 00:12:26,587
A delete, a sent email and a wired payment
cannot.

273
00:12:26,637 --> 00:12:29,109
Those three of five need a human approval
gate.

274
00:12:29,159 --> 00:12:33,312
In a case, an action that cannot be
undone, with no human check, is not

275
00:12:33,362 --> 00:12:33,593
appropriate.

276
00:12:33,643 --> 00:12:37,796
For example, an agent drafting a cover
letter needs no gate, since the draft can

277
00:12:37,846 --> 00:12:38,637
be thrown away.

278
00:12:38,687 --> 00:12:42,560
Submitting the application cannot be taken
back, so the job seeker approves it first.

279
00:12:42,910 --> 00:12:46,988
Audit trail clarity asks whether the AI's
actions leave a trail you can review

280
00:12:47,038 --> 00:12:47,283
afterwards.

281
00:12:47,333 --> 00:12:50,232
In the left log, three of three actions
are attributed.

282
00:12:50,282 --> 00:12:52,001
In the right, zero of three.

283
00:12:52,051 --> 00:12:54,361
Reversibility is about undoing, this is
about knowing.

284
00:12:54,411 --> 00:12:58,489
In a case, without a clear trail nobody
can evaluate what the AI did.

285
00:12:58,539 --> 00:13:02,912
For example, an agent and a job seeker
both edit her online profile, and nothing

286
00:13:02,962 --> 00:13:04,092
records who changed what.

287
00:13:04,142 --> 00:13:08,220
Afterwards nobody can tell who rewrote her
headline, so that editing should not be

288
00:13:08,270 --> 00:13:08,810
delegated yet.

289
00:13:09,160 --> 00:13:12,941
This plot sets the override rate, how
often humans disagree, against the audited

290
00:13:12,991 --> 00:13:15,298
error rate, how often the AI is wrong.

291
00:13:15,348 --> 00:13:18,834
Calibrated trust means reliance matches
the AI's actual reliability for this kind

292
00:13:18,884 --> 00:13:19,423
of case.

293
00:13:19,473 --> 00:13:20,897
That is the dashed diagonal.

294
00:13:20,947 --> 00:13:25,906
In a case, this is how you judge whoever
is evaluating the AI, and whether they do

295
00:13:25,956 --> 00:13:26,495
it well.

296
00:13:26,545 --> 00:13:30,326
For example, a recruiter overrides a
résumé screener about as often as audits

297
00:13:30,376 --> 00:13:31,505
show it is wrong.

298
00:13:31,555 --> 00:13:35,335
Where audits show it errs on unusual
career paths, she checks those more

299
00:13:35,385 --> 00:13:35,630
closely.

300
00:13:35,980 --> 00:13:38,397
A red dot appears high above the diagonal.

301
00:13:38,447 --> 00:13:41,481
Undertrust means systematically
discounting the AI's output even when it

302
00:13:41,531 --> 00:13:43,640
is reliable, so its value is lost.

303
00:13:43,690 --> 00:13:47,649
In this made-up example the AI is rarely
wrong, yet twenty-four percent is

304
00:13:47,699 --> 00:13:47,957
overridden.

305
00:13:48,007 --> 00:13:51,967
Here the human evaluator is too harsh, and
good work is thrown away.

306
00:13:52,017 --> 00:13:56,284
For example, a job seeker rewrites every
résumé the agent tailors, though her own

307
00:13:56,334 --> 00:13:57,826
checks keep finding them correct.

308
00:13:57,876 --> 00:14:00,910
The hours the agent was meant to save are
lost.

309
00:14:01,260 --> 00:14:03,358
A red dot sits below the diagonal.

310
00:14:03,408 --> 00:14:07,039
Overtrust means accepting AI output that
should be checked, so errors surface

311
00:14:07,089 --> 00:14:07,652
later, quietly.

312
00:14:07,702 --> 00:14:11,641
In this practice case, humans overrode
seven percent, but a small audit found

313
00:14:11,691 --> 00:14:12,561
fifteen percent wrong.

314
00:14:12,611 --> 00:14:13,788
A signal, not proof.

315
00:14:13,838 --> 00:14:17,470
Here the human check exists, but it may be
letting errors through.

316
00:14:17,520 --> 00:14:21,458
For example, a job seeker sends every
cover letter the agent drafts without

317
00:14:21,508 --> 00:14:22,071
reading it.

318
00:14:22,121 --> 00:14:26,366
A letter that praises the wrong company
comes to light only when a recruiter

319
00:14:26,416 --> 00:14:26,980
mentions it.

320
00:14:27,330 --> 00:14:29,621
Bar A and bar B, fifty papers each.

321
00:14:29,671 --> 00:14:32,547
One was corrected in bar A, fourteen in
bar B.

322
00:14:32,597 --> 00:14:36,936
The monitoring gap is the distance between
the data a deployment holds and the data

323
00:14:36,986 --> 00:14:37,521
anyone reviews.

324
00:14:37,571 --> 00:14:38,985
Looking is the cheap fix.

325
00:14:39,035 --> 00:14:42,203
In a case, ask whether anyone evaluates
the record at all.

326
00:14:42,253 --> 00:14:46,300
For example, an agent's log records every
application and which ones led to an

327
00:14:46,350 --> 00:14:46,593
interview.

328
00:14:46,642 --> 00:14:50,982
If nobody looks at it, the job seeker
never sees that résumés tailored for some

329
00:14:51,032 --> 00:14:53,030
kinds of role never get a reply.

330
00:14:53,380 --> 00:14:57,002
This table fills each disclosure field in
a vague way and a complete way.

331
00:14:57,052 --> 00:15:01,460
An AI Use Disclosure is a per-step account
of how AI was used, the tool, the request,

332
00:15:01,510 --> 00:15:03,559
the output, what was checked and what
changed.

333
00:15:03,609 --> 00:15:06,706
In a case, it is where you find what
exactly was checked.

334
00:15:06,756 --> 00:15:10,640
For example, for a cover letter, a job
seeker names the model, her request and

335
00:15:10,690 --> 00:15:11,689
the draft it gave.

336
00:15:11,739 --> 00:15:15,623
She notes that she checked every claim
about the company, and cut a sentence that

337
00:15:15,673 --> 00:15:16,410
overstated her experience.

338
00:15:16,760 --> 00:15:18,185
Four properties, checkbox against log.

339
00:15:18,235 --> 00:15:22,609
A supervisory log is the disclosure taken
as a whole, showing where the person used

340
00:15:22,659 --> 00:15:23,199
each capacity.

341
00:15:23,249 --> 00:15:25,853
It is granular, honest, time-stamped and
tied to evidence.

342
00:15:25,903 --> 00:15:27,623
A checkbox passes zero of four.

343
00:15:27,673 --> 00:15:32,637
In a case, the log is the evidence that
the checks happened, not just a claim that

344
00:15:32,687 --> 00:15:33,227
they did.

345
00:15:33,277 --> 00:15:36,766
For example, across a month of
applications, a job seeker's log shows

346
00:15:36,816 --> 00:15:41,190
each check, with its date, including the
time she caught the agent listing a degree

347
00:15:41,240 --> 00:15:42,370
she does not hold.

348
00:15:42,720 --> 00:15:45,362
Six components, twenty steps each, all
made up.

349
00:15:45,412 --> 00:15:48,726
Hierarchical mapping means mapping by
component, with step-by-step detail only

350
00:15:48,776 --> 00:15:49,736
at high-stakes seams.

351
00:15:49,786 --> 00:15:51,755
Red components have every step mapped.

352
00:15:51,805 --> 00:15:53,774
Grey ones get one policy line.

353
00:15:53,824 --> 00:15:56,129
Forty-four entries instead of one hundred
twenty.

354
00:15:56,179 --> 00:16:00,503
In a case, the contract should be most
detailed where authority changes hands.

355
00:16:00,553 --> 00:16:04,541
For example, a job-search agent's
searching and sorting get a policy line

356
00:16:04,591 --> 00:16:04,877
each.

357
00:16:04,927 --> 00:16:08,915
Submitting an application in the job
seeker's name, where authority passes to

358
00:16:08,965 --> 00:16:11,270
the agent, is mapped step by step.

359
00:16:11,620 --> 00:16:13,828
Now pairs that are easy to mix up.

360
00:16:13,878 --> 00:16:15,522
On the left, a broken pipeline.

361
00:16:15,572 --> 00:16:20,322
It lacks one of items one to four, so the
bar halts and the gap announces itself.

362
00:16:20,372 --> 00:16:22,015
On the right, an unsupervised pipeline.

363
00:16:22,065 --> 00:16:26,815
It lacks one of items five to eight, runs
to the end, and the errors go unseen.

364
00:16:26,865 --> 00:16:29,920
One lacks the standard documentation, the
other lacks the supervisory additions.

365
00:16:30,270 --> 00:16:30,873
Next pair.

366
00:16:30,923 --> 00:16:34,139
Undertrust, on the left, is a reliable AI
being discounted.

367
00:16:34,189 --> 00:16:37,404
In the sketch, four of ten good outputs
are rejected.

368
00:16:37,454 --> 00:16:40,670
Overtrust, on the right, is an unreliable
AI accepted unchecked.

369
00:16:40,720 --> 00:16:43,609
Three of ten outputs are wrong, and waved
through.

370
00:16:43,659 --> 00:16:44,915
Undertrust wastes good work.

371
00:16:44,965 --> 00:16:46,548
Overtrust lets bad work pass.

372
00:16:46,598 --> 00:16:49,160
Both are the evaluator failing, in
opposite directions.

373
00:16:49,510 --> 00:16:50,012
Third pair.

374
00:16:50,062 --> 00:16:53,603
The delegation map, on the left, specifies
what should happen, here four steps.

375
00:16:53,653 --> 00:16:56,365
The audit trail, on the right, shows what
did happen.

376
00:16:56,415 --> 00:16:59,127
Step three is missing, so only three of
four happened.

377
00:16:59,177 --> 00:17:01,613
Comparing the two is how a deviation gets
noticed.

378
00:17:01,663 --> 00:17:05,480
The map is the contract, the trail is what
an evaluator checks it against.

379
00:17:05,830 --> 00:17:06,338
Last pair.

380
00:17:06,388 --> 00:17:10,247
Verification cost is the effort to check
an output, here four minutes of checking

381
00:17:10,297 --> 00:17:11,643
for forty minutes of work.

382
00:17:11,693 --> 00:17:14,435
It is high only when checking is nearly a
redo.

383
00:17:14,485 --> 00:17:18,065
Stakes is the cost if the output is wrong,
one or five hundred.

384
00:17:18,115 --> 00:17:21,136
A task can be cheap to check and still
high stakes.

385
00:17:21,186 --> 00:17:23,370
Both help decide where a human check
belongs.

386
00:17:23,720 --> 00:17:26,248
A vague request, its exact version, and a
chart.

387
00:17:26,298 --> 00:17:29,973
The phrase, a human reviews it, becomes,
tick three edge-pattern checks, pick one

388
00:17:30,023 --> 00:17:32,837
of four codes, and mid scores get a second
officer.

389
00:17:32,887 --> 00:17:35,416
In the simulated run, that is twenty-five
of forty.

390
00:17:35,466 --> 00:17:39,140
Now the case says what exactly the check
is, and who does it.

391
00:17:39,490 --> 00:17:44,029
The question, is the AI trustworthy here,
becomes, compare the override rate with an

392
00:17:44,079 --> 00:17:46,323
audited error rate from the same stream.

393
00:17:46,373 --> 00:17:49,928
In the practice case, overrides are seven
percent, audited errors fifteen.

394
00:17:49,978 --> 00:17:52,550
Overrides run at about half the error
rate.

395
00:17:52,600 --> 00:17:55,500
That exact comparison is how you judge the
evaluator.

396
00:17:55,850 --> 00:17:59,619
The question, should the AI do this step,
becomes, answer the five AI-or-human

397
00:17:59,669 --> 00:18:02,263
questions for the sub-task and write the
sorting down.

398
00:18:02,313 --> 00:18:05,201
For wiring a payment, three of five
squares are red.

399
00:18:05,251 --> 00:18:07,257
Under the made-up rule here, human only.

400
00:18:07,307 --> 00:18:11,370
That is how a case shows whether a step is
appropriate for the AI.

401
00:18:11,720 --> 00:18:15,780
The claim, the pipeline is documented,
becomes, all eight map items for every

402
00:18:15,830 --> 00:18:17,676
step, including the four supervisory ones.

403
00:18:17,726 --> 00:18:21,470
In the squares, item six is crossed out,
leaving seven of eight.

404
00:18:21,520 --> 00:18:23,999
It runs, but it is an unsupervised
pipeline.

405
00:18:24,049 --> 00:18:27,160
That is how you judge whether the contract
is complete.

406
00:18:27,510 --> 00:18:31,578
The request, say that AI was used,
becomes, per step, the tool, the request,

407
00:18:31,628 --> 00:18:33,932
the output, what was checked and what
changed.

408
00:18:33,982 --> 00:18:35,697
That is an AI Use Disclosure.

409
00:18:35,747 --> 00:18:39,227
The squares show four of twenty entries
checked again, one in five.

410
00:18:39,277 --> 00:18:41,580
Now the record says exactly what was
checked.

411
00:18:41,930 --> 00:18:44,527
A closing checklist of six checks, ticked
in turn.

412
00:18:44,577 --> 00:18:47,175
Read it as the routine for any delegation
case.

413
00:18:47,225 --> 00:18:48,645
It starts with the contract.

414
00:18:48,695 --> 00:18:52,469
Ask whether a stranger could tell, from
the log alone, whether each handoff

415
00:18:52,519 --> 00:18:53,352
condition was met.

416
00:18:53,402 --> 00:18:57,470
That is what testable means, and it is a
test of an appropriate contract.

417
00:18:57,820 --> 00:18:59,113
Second, still the contract.

418
00:18:59,163 --> 00:19:03,815
Check that all eight map items are in
place, especially items five to eight.

419
00:19:03,865 --> 00:19:08,180
Those are the supervisory additions, such
as failure routing, the audit trail and

420
00:19:08,230 --> 00:19:08,852
sign-off authority.

421
00:19:08,902 --> 00:19:12,210
Without them the pipeline still runs, as
an unsupervised pipeline.

422
00:19:12,560 --> 00:19:14,707
Third, the checks behind the steps.

423
00:19:14,757 --> 00:19:17,636
Check that each supervisory capacity
leaves an artifact.

424
00:19:17,686 --> 00:19:19,467
That is its operational form.

425
00:19:19,517 --> 00:19:21,664
Plausibility auditing leaves an audit log.

426
00:19:21,714 --> 00:19:23,861
Problem formulation leaves a scoping
document.

427
00:19:23,911 --> 00:19:26,058
Tool orchestration leaves the delegation
map.

428
00:19:26,108 --> 00:19:29,720
No artifact, no capacity, and no evidence
the check happened.

429
00:19:30,070 --> 00:19:31,325
Fourth, the phase gates.

430
00:19:31,375 --> 00:19:35,240
Ask where the checks are and who does
them, AI or human.

431
00:19:35,290 --> 00:19:40,135
Ask whether a missing human check, or a
missing AI check, is appropriate, and what

432
00:19:40,185 --> 00:19:41,440
exactly each check covers.

433
00:19:41,490 --> 00:19:44,376
The five questions, verification cost,
stakes, distribution match, reversibility

434
00:19:44,426 --> 00:19:46,660
and audit trail clarity, set each gate.

435
00:19:47,010 --> 00:19:47,976
Fifth, who evaluates.

436
00:19:48,026 --> 00:19:51,364
Ask whether the override rate matches the
audited error rate.

437
00:19:51,414 --> 00:19:54,074
If it does, that points to calibrated
trust.

438
00:19:54,124 --> 00:19:56,445
Many more overrides than errors suggests
undertrust.

439
00:19:56,495 --> 00:19:57,800
Far fewer suggests overtrust.

440
00:19:57,850 --> 00:20:00,510
If nobody looks, that is a monitoring gap.

441
00:20:00,860 --> 00:20:04,693
Last, check that the disclosure shows what
was checked and what was corrected.

442
00:20:04,743 --> 00:20:05,888
Then say it exactly.

443
00:20:05,938 --> 00:20:09,771
Not, a human reviews it, but three ticks
and one of four codes.

444
00:20:09,821 --> 00:20:12,160
The exam expects the exact term as well.

445
00:20:13,010 --> 00:20:13,491
Let's recap.

446
00:20:13,541 --> 00:20:17,474
First ask whether the case has a contract,
or only a split of the work.

447
00:20:17,524 --> 00:20:20,661
An appropriate contract has testable
handoffs and a declared route for failure.

448
00:20:20,711 --> 00:20:23,582
Phase gates and delegation buckets show
whether the steps are appropriate.

449
00:20:23,632 --> 00:20:27,034
Find every check, who does it, and the
artifact that proves it happened.

450
00:20:27,084 --> 00:20:30,487
Judge the evaluator by comparing the
override rate with the audited error rate.

451
00:20:30,537 --> 00:20:34,470
Then say it exactly, three ticks and one
of four codes, not a vague review.

452
00:20:34,820 --> 00:20:37,650
Give me short delegation cases, one at a
time.

453
00:20:37,700 --> 00:20:39,570
For each, I answer four questions.

454
00:20:39,620 --> 00:20:42,130
Is there a contract, and is it
appropriate?

455
00:20:42,180 --> 00:20:43,410
Are the steps appropriate?

456
00:20:43,460 --> 00:20:45,970
Where are the checks, and who does them?

457
00:20:46,020 --> 00:20:47,890
Who evaluates, and is that appropriate?

458
00:20:47,940 --> 00:20:51,410
I must name the exact terms, handoff
condition, testable, failure routing,

459
00:20:51,460 --> 00:20:53,970
delegation buckets, verification cost,
unsupervised pipeline, sign-off authority

460
00:20:54,020 --> 00:20:54,610
and overtrust.

461
00:20:54,660 --> 00:20:58,770
Hold back your answer until I reply, then
correct me in two sentences.

462
00:20:59,120 --> 00:21:01,253
Key Terms: Delegation and Trust.

463
00:21:01,303 --> 00:21:03,000
At Nik Bear Brown.
